WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.262
TABBI helps people organize browser tabs, and return to the materials they need.

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:07.735
As we switch between multiple tasks,

00:00:07.988 --> 00:00:09.954
browser tabs keep accumulating.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.331
We leave them open to read later,

00:00:12.627 --> 00:00:16.632
remember unfinished work, or avoid losing useful materials.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.954
But without organization, we can forget why we opened a page,

00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:23.120
or what we intended to do with it.

00:00:23.427 --> 00:00:25.889
The goal is not simply to close tabs.

00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:27.981
It is to group them by purpose,

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:31.383
while keeping the links and context that help us return.

00:00:31.774 --> 00:00:33.602
TABBI connects two activities:

00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:35.779
organizing and saving tabs,

00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:37.829
then finding and reopening them.

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:42.468
Saved task names, links, and notes connect the two.

00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:44.824
Here is the actual Chrome extension.

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:47.329
It reads the tabs in the current window.

00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:51.143
The user describes their work and requests organization.

00:00:51.619 --> 00:00:54.725
TABBI suggests task groups based on tab titles,

00:00:54.915 --> 00:00:57.166
links, and saved tasks.

00:00:57.409 --> 00:00:59.449
The user reviews these suggestions,

00:00:59.702 --> 00:01:02.841
renames a group, and moves tabs between tasks.

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:05.524
They can add materials to an existing task

00:01:05.736 --> 00:01:07.384
or keep them as a new task.

00:01:07.881 --> 00:01:09.178
The proposal remains editable.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:12.094
A note can record what to do next.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.089
The user saves the selected tasks before closing their tabs.

00:01:16.554 --> 00:01:18.181
Other tabs remain open.

00:01:18.564 --> 00:01:22.357
Later, the user asks for saved tabs about Generative UI.

00:01:22.886 --> 00:01:27.683
TABBI recommends matching pages and asks whether to focus on papers or include

00:01:27.694 --> 00:01:29.500
workshop materials and notes.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:33.336
The user can choose a suggested option or add a condition.

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:36.506
Here, the user asks to see only papers.

00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:38.514
TABBI narrows the results.

00:01:38.873 --> 00:01:41.610
The user selects the relevant pages and opens them.

00:01:42.096 --> 00:01:44.051
These are the actual saved pages,

00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:46.450
rather than a simulated restored list.

00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:49.165
The workflow starts with open tabs.

00:01:49.408 --> 00:01:53.064
The model suggests groups, and the user reviews and saves them.

00:01:53.603 --> 00:01:56.868
Task names, links, and notes preserve the context.

00:01:57.291 --> 00:02:01.402
When the user returns, the agent uses that context to interpret their purpose,

00:02:01.761 --> 00:02:03.230
asks a question if needed,

00:02:03.462 --> 00:02:05.132
and updates its recommendation.

00:02:05.586 --> 00:02:07.668
The user chooses what to reopen.

00:02:07.932 --> 00:02:11.503
All three participants responded positively to automatic grouping.

00:02:11.968 --> 00:02:15.667
They valued suggestions when they were unsure how to organize their materials,

00:02:15.963 --> 00:02:19.280
and appreciated being able to review and edit the groups.

00:02:19.365 --> 00:02:22.493
But agent assistance was not preferred in every situation.

00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:27.755
Participant one found that browsing the list helped rediscover forgotten materials.

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:31.464
Conversation required remembering what they wanted to find.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:34.993
Participant two used a broad goal, planning a trip,

00:02:35.247 --> 00:02:38.618
and appreciated suggestions for maps and AI tools.

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:42.644
Participant three preferred direct browsing with fewer than ten tabs,

00:02:42.918 --> 00:02:45.232
and saw repeated questions as extra work.

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:48.846
Suggested buttons could still provide useful options.

00:02:49.015 --> 00:02:53.855
Automatic grouping helped, but conversational retrieval was not always the easiest way back.

00:02:54.373 --> 00:02:59.318
Users should be able to choose between talking to the agent and browsing their saved tabs.

00:02:59.772 --> 00:03:02.530
The design lesson is to reduce difficult decisions,

00:03:02.742 --> 00:03:04.929
without adding unnecessary steps.

00:03:05.415 --> 00:03:06.461
Thank you for watching.
